/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · psychology batch Y: The Mind */
WB.L("inattentional-blindness", "psychology", "beginner", "The invisible gorilla: why we miss what's in front of us",
  "Half the people in a famous experiment failed to notice a gorilla walking through a basketball game. They weren't careless. They were human, and so are you.",
  [
    ["The gorilla nobody saw", "When we focus hard on one thing, we can miss something huge and obvious.",
      "In 1999, psychologists Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris made a short video of students passing basketballs. Viewers were asked to count the passes made by the team in white shirts. Partway through, a person in a gorilla suit strolls into the middle of the game, thumps their chest and walks off. It is on screen for several seconds. Roughly half the viewers never saw it.\n\nWhen told afterwards, many refused to believe it until they watched again, and some accused the researchers of swapping the tape. The study has been repeated many times with similar results. The lesson is unsettling: we do not see everything our eyes are pointed at."],
    ["Attention is a spotlight", "Attention picks out a small slice of what reaches your eyes, so we can miss unexpected objects and even big changes.",
      "It feels as though we take in a whole scene in rich detail, like a camera. We do not. Your eyes send the brain more than it can process, so attention acts like a spotlight, picking out what seems relevant and leaving the rest dim. Psychologists call failing to notice an unexpected object while focused on something else inattentional blindness, a term popularised by Arien Mack and Irvin Rock. The missed object is in plain view. What is missing is attention turning light into conscious experience.\n\nA close cousin is change blindness. In a 1998 study by Simons and Daniel Levin, pedestrians giving directions had the researcher swapped for a different person behind a passing door. Around half did not notice. Film continuity errors work the same way."],
    ["Even experts miss gorillas", "Expertise sharpens what you look for, which can blind you to what you don't expect.",
      "You might think trained professionals would be immune. In a 2013 study, Trafton Drew, Melissa Vo and Jeremy Wolfe asked radiologists to search lung scans for small nodules that can signal cancer. In the final case, the researchers had inserted a small image of a gorilla, many times larger than a typical nodule. About four in five radiologists did not report it, even though eye tracking showed most had looked directly at the spot.\n\nThis does not mean radiologists are bad at their job. They were brilliantly tuned to find nodules, and that is the point. Expertise tells you where to look and what to look for, and anything outside that search template can slip by unseen."],
    ["Looked but failed to see", "On the road, inattentional blindness can be deadly, and hands-free calls do not remove the risk.",
      "Road safety researchers have a name for a common type of crash: 'looked but failed to see'. A driver pulls out of a side street and hits a motorcyclist or cyclist, then insists they checked and the road was clear. Often they are telling the truth as they experienced it. Their attention was tuned to cars, so a smaller, less expected vehicle did not register.\n\nResearch by David Strayer and colleagues has found that phone conversations make this worse, and that hands-free calls are still distracting, because the problem is divided attention, not just your hands. Australian road rules on phones vary by state, so check your state transport authority, but the psychology is the same everywhere."],
    ["Designing around blind spots", "Selective attention is a useful filter, and since knowing about it doesn't switch it off, the answer is building habits and systems.",
      "Selective attention is what lets you function at all. Imagine reading this sentence while consciously processing every sound, the feel of your clothes and every object in your peripheral vision. Filtering lets you hold a conversation in a noisy cafe. The cost is that it sometimes throws out something important. People told about the gorilla in advance nearly always see it, but in a later Simons variation, those who knew the original gorilla were no better at spotting other unexpected changes.\n\nSo confidence is not evidence. When driving, make a deliberate second look for bikes, motorbikes and pedestrians. For important work, use checklists or a second pair of eyes. Allow that two honest people can see different things. The gorilla is always there somewhere; build systems that catch it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the gorilla experiment show?", ["Focused attention can hide something large and obvious","Most people have poor eyesight without glasses","People can count and notice everything at once","Gorillas are easy to spot in a crowd of players"], 0, "We do not see everything our eyes are pointed at when attention is elsewhere."],
    [1, "What causes inattentional blindness?", ["The object is hidden from view by other objects in the scene","Attention does not turn what reaches your eyes into awareness","The eyes fail to receive light from the object in the first place","Viewers are not paying any attention to the scene at all"], 1, "The missed object is in plain view; what is lacking is attention, not light."],
    [2, "Why did most radiologists miss the gorilla in the scan?", ["Their expertise tuned them to nodules, so a gorilla slipped by","They were not looking at that part of the scan at all","They are generally poor at finding the nodules they search for","They did not take the research task seriously enough"], 0, "Expertise directs attention, and unexpected items outside the search template can be missed."],
    [3, "Why are hands-free calls still risky for drivers?", ["The phone signal interferes with the car","The problem is divided attention, not only the hands","Hands-free kits are poorly made and often faulty","Drivers tend to talk louder on hands-free calls"], 1, "Attention, not hand position, is the limited resource."],
    [4, "What helps most with the limits of selective attention?", ["Know about the gorilla and you'll always spot it","Build habits and systems such as checklists and second looks","Concentrate harder on everything at the same time","Trust your confidence that you would have noticed"], 1, "Awareness alone doesn't switch the effect off, so we build in safeguards."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Half of viewers missed a gorilla in plain sight. Experts did too."});

WB.L("cognitive-dissonance", "psychology", "intermediate", "Cognitive dissonance: why we twist the facts to feel better",
  "When reality clashes with what we believe or what we've done, something has to give. Surprisingly often, it isn't the belief.",
  [
    ["A prophecy fails", "A doomsday group whose prophecy failed gave psychologists a view of how people defend a belief.",
      "In 1954, a small group in the American Midwest believed a flood would destroy much of the world on 21 December, and that a flying saucer would rescue the faithful beforehand. Some members quit jobs and gave away possessions. Unknown to the group, several new members were researchers working with psychologist Leon Festinger.\n\nThey watched as midnight passed with no spaceship and no flood. Rather than abandon the belief, the leader announced that the group's faith had saved the world, and some members began seeking publicity and recruits for the first time. Festinger and colleagues described the episode in the 1956 book When Prophecy Fails. It illustrated an idea Festinger would soon make famous."],
    ["What dissonance is", "Holding clashing thoughts creates discomfort that we are driven to reduce, often by changing beliefs rather than behaviour.",
      "In 1957 Festinger published A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance. When we hold two beliefs, or a belief and an action, that conflict, we feel an uncomfortable tension. A smoker who knows smoking kills experiences it every time they light up.\n\nThat discomfort motivates us to restore consistency, and there are only a few ways. We can change our behaviour, by quitting. We can change our belief, by deciding the risks are exaggerated. Or we can add thoughts that soften the clash, such as 'my grandfather smoked and lived to ninety' or 'everyone dies of something'. Changing behaviour is often the hardest option, so the mind quietly reaches for the others."],
    ["Justifying our choices", "After a hard decision or a costly effort, we tend to value what we chose or suffered for more.",
      "Dissonance is thought to be strongest after a hard decision. If you agonised between two similar cars and bought one, you may suddenly notice how good your choice is and how flawed the other was. In 1956 Jack Brehm had people choose between two appliances they liked equally; the chosen item tended to rise in their ratings, the rejected one to fall. Later researchers noted a statistical problem with this design, and corrected studies often find a smaller effect. In a 1968 racetrack study, punters who had just placed a bet were more confident of winning.\n\nThe same logic gives effort justification. In a 1959 study, Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills found that people who endured an embarrassing initiation rated a dull group more highly. It helps explain fierce loyalty after hazing, and why people stay with failing projects."],
    ["Debates and doubts", "The theory is influential, but classic experiments deserve more caution than textbooks usually give them.",
      "The most famous dissonance study is a 1959 experiment by Festinger and James Carlsmith. Students did an hour of dull tasks, then were paid one dollar or twenty to tell the next participant it was fun. The one dollar group later rated the task as more enjoyable. The explanation: twenty dollars was reason enough to lie, one dollar was not, so they decided the task wasn't so bad. Replications of this kind of finding have been mixed, so it deserves caution.\n\nThe theory has always had critics. In the 1960s Daryl Bem argued that we simply infer our attitudes from our behaviour. Others suggested the real driver is protecting our self-image as a good, sensible person. The doomsday study is also questioned. Most psychologists still accept that people often rationalise, but treat specific classic experiments cautiously."],
    ["Using dissonance wisely", "Noticing your own defensiveness can help you think more honestly, and offering others a face-saving way to change their mind works better than cornering them.",
      "The practical value of dissonance theory is self-awareness. When you feel defensive, irritated or eager to explain something away, a cherished belief may have met an inconvenient fact. Pausing to ask 'what would change my mind?' helps. So does separating your identity from your opinions: being wrong about a decision does not make you foolish.\n\nIn relationships, remember that others feel dissonance too, so cornering someone with evidence often makes them dig in. Offering a face-saving way to change their mind works better. There is a famous anecdote from Benjamin Franklin, who said he won over a rival by asking to borrow a rare book. Helping you, perhaps, made the rival like you more."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the failed doomsday prophecy illustrate?", ["Believers always give up their belief when proven wrong","Groups quickly forget failed predictions and move on","People may defend a belief harder when reality contradicts it","Prophecies about the end of the world usually come true"], 2, "The group reinterpreted the failure rather than abandoning the belief."],
    [1, "What is cognitive dissonance?", ["The inability to make any decision between two close options","The feeling of complete certainty that follows a decision","The discomfort of clashing beliefs or actions, which we try to ease","The fear of being found wrong in front of other people"], 2, "The tension motivates us to restore consistency, often by changing beliefs."],
    [2, "What is 'effort justification'?", ["Spending effort to justify our decisions to other people","Rewarding people strictly in proportion to their effort","Valuing something more because of the effort it cost us","Avoiding tasks that would require too much effort"], 2, "Admitting we suffered for something worthless creates dissonance, so the mind upgrades the prize."],
    [3, "What does the lesson say about the classic dissonance experiments?", ["They deserve more caution than textbooks give them","They have all been conclusively proven by replication","They have all been fully rejected by later researchers","They are the only real evidence for the whole theory"], 0, "Science updates its beliefs, and some famous findings have not replicated cleanly."],
    [4, "What works best when helping someone change their mind?", ["Cornering them with evidence","Offering a face-saving way to change it","Repeating the facts more loudly","Pointing out their earlier mistakes"], 1, "People feel dissonance too, and being cornered makes them dig in."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A prophecy failed on schedule. Its believers got stronger. Why?"});

WB.L("impostor-phenomenon", "psychology", "beginner", "Impostor feelings: when success feels like a fluke",
  "You got the job, passed the exam or won the award, and you're quietly waiting to be found out. You are in very good company.",
  [
    ["Waiting to be found out", "Many capable people secretly believe they don't deserve their success, a pattern named in 1978 as the impostor phenomenon.",
      "Picture a doctor newly qualified, a manager freshly promoted, or a student who topped the class. From the outside they look confident. Inside runs a different story: 'I got lucky. Any day now, someone will realise I don't know what I'm doing.' This feeling of being a fraud despite clear evidence of competence is widely known as impostor syndrome.\n\nThe concept was introduced in 1978 by American psychologists Pauline Rose Clance and Suzanne Imes, working with high-achieving women who credited luck or charm rather than ability. Note the word phenomenon, not syndrome. It is not a mental illness or a diagnosis. It describes thoughts and feelings many ordinary, healthy people experience at times. Later research found it in men as well, across many professions and cultures."],
    ["How common is it?", "Impostor feelings are clearly widespread, but claims such as '70 per cent' have murky origins.",
      "You may have seen claims that 70 per cent of people experience impostor feelings. That figure is repeated often, but its origins are murky. A 2020 systematic review led by Dena Bravata found reported rates from under ten per cent to over eighty per cent, depending on who was studied and how it was measured.\n\nThe honest conclusion is that nobody knows the exact figure, but impostor feelings are very common, particularly during transitions such as starting university, a new job or a promotion. They seem especially frequent among people who are among the first in their family or community to reach a particular position, and in competitive fields where everyone around you seems brilliant."],
    ["The impostor cycle", "Overpreparing or procrastinating, then explaining success away, keeps the doubt alive.",
      "Clance described a self-sustaining loop sometimes called the impostor cycle. Faced with a task, a person feels anxious and doubtful. They respond in one of two ways: by overpreparing, working far longer than necessary, or by procrastinating, then working frantically at the last minute.\n\nThe task goes well. But instead of concluding 'I'm good at this', they explain the success away. If they overprepared, it was only the extra effort. If they procrastinated, it was luck. Either way, the success does not count as evidence of ability, so the doubt is ready and waiting for the next task. Breaking the cycle often starts with noticing how you explain your wins, and whether you would accept that explanation from a friend."],
    ["Not a skill problem", "Impostor feelings come from how success is interpreted, often in competent people, and sometimes from real signals in the environment.",
      "Impostor feelings are sometimes described as the opposite of the Dunning-Kruger effect, the idea that people with low skill overestimate themselves. The comparison is catchy but loose. People with impostor feelings are usually genuinely competent. Expertise can even feed the feeling: the more you learn, the more you see how much you don't know, while beginners may be blissfully unaware of the gaps.\n\nSome researchers also criticise the 'syndrome' label for putting all the focus on individuals. If you are the only person like you in a room, hear subtle comments questioning whether you belong, or never see anyone with your background in senior roles, feeling out of place may be a reasonable response to real signals. Leaders should ask what the environment is communicating, not just tell people to be more confident."],
    ["Quieting the inner critic", "Talking about it, keeping a record of evidence and finding mentors help you see your achievements more accurately.",
      "Several simple habits loosen the grip of impostor feelings. Talk about it: hearing that respected colleagues feel the same is powerfully reassuring. Keep a record of positive feedback, results and things you have learned, and reread it when doubts flare. Separate feelings from facts by asking, 'What evidence would convince me I'm competent?' and checking whether it already exists. Accept that not knowing everything is normal at every level. Mentors can tell you what 'good' looks like in your field.\n\nIf self-doubt is persistent, affecting your sleep or mood, or stopping you from going for opportunities, it is worth speaking to your GP. This is general information, not personal advice, and healthdirect and Beyond Blue have useful resources."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is 'impostor phenomenon' the more accurate term than 'syndrome'?", ["It only affects people working in certain kinds of jobs","It is a pattern of thoughts and feelings, not a diagnosis","It affects only women, and never affects men in any field","It is a clinical condition that always requires treatment"], 1, "Many ordinary, healthy people experience impostor feelings at times."],
    [1, "What does research say about how common impostor feelings are?", ["Exactly 70 per cent of people experience them at some time","They are rare outside the medical and legal professions","They affect fewer than one in ten people in any group","Reported rates vary hugely, so the exact figure is unknown"], 3, "Rates range from under ten to over eighty per cent depending on who and how it was measured."],
    [2, "What keeps the impostor cycle going?", ["Failing at nearly every task the person attempts","Avoiding every challenging piece of work altogether","Receiving too much praise from colleagues and managers","Explaining success away as effort or luck rather than ability"], 3, "If success never counts as evidence of ability, the doubt returns with the next task."],
    [3, "Why can expertise feed impostor feelings?", ["Experts get far less feedback than beginners do","Experts are usually less skilled than they appear","The more you know, the more you see what you don't","Beginners always know more about their own gaps"], 2, "Feeling unsure can reflect a realistic understanding of how complex the work is."],
    [4, "Which habit helps loosen impostor feelings?", ["Avoid any situation where other people might judge your work","Work far longer than necessary on every single task","Keep a record of your wins and reread it when doubts flare","Keep the feelings to yourself rather than talking about them"], 2, "Evidence of competence is easier to see when you record it and review it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"You earned the job, so why does it feel like a fluke? You are not alone."});

WB.L("spotlight-effect", "psychology", "beginner", "The spotlight effect: nobody's watching as closely as you think",
  "That embarrassing stumble, the stain on your shirt, the awkward thing you said: you're replaying it all night. Everyone else has already forgotten.",
  [
    ["The T-shirt experiment", "People overestimate how much others notice them, a bias called the spotlight effect.",
      "In a 2000 study, psychologist Thomas Gilovich and colleagues at Cornell University had students wear a T-shirt with a large picture of Barry Manilow, a singer then considered deeply uncool. Each wearer walked briefly into a room of peers filling in questionnaires, then left. Asked how many people had noticed the shirt, they guessed about half. In reality, only about a quarter could say who was on it. The researchers called this the spotlight effect: we believe we are noticed, for better or worse, far more than we really are."],
    ["Why we feel spotlit", "We judge how we look to others from our own intense inner view and don't adjust enough, while observers read mistakes in context and move on.",
      "You are the one person you can never escape. You feel every flush of embarrassment and replay every clumsy sentence in high definition. When guessing what others see, you start from that inner view and adjust, but researchers argue we don't adjust nearly enough. Everyone else is busy with their own spotlight.\n\nStudies by Kenneth Savitsky, Nicholas Epley and Gilovich found people who committed a minor blunder in front of others expected harsher judgement than observers gave. Observers see the wider context and remember their own slips. While you rerun your blunder at 2 am, the witnesses have most likely moved on."],
    ["The illusion of transparency", "We overestimate how visible our nerves are, and learning this makes speakers calmer and better.",
      "A close cousin is the illusion of transparency: believing our inner states show more than they do. In late 1990s experiments, liars overestimated how many people would catch them, and people tasting a foul drink overestimated how obvious their disgust was. A speaker may feel a pounding heart and wobbling voice and assume the audience sees terror. Usually they see someone mildly nervous.\n\nThere is good news. In a 2003 study by Savitsky and Gilovich, students told about this illusion felt less anxious and gave speeches that observers rated better than those of students who were simply reassured. Knowing the bias breaks the loop of worry feeding worry."],
    ["The liking gap", "After conversations, people underestimate how much the other person liked them.",
      "The same pattern shapes friendships. In 2018, Erica Boothby, Gus Cooney and colleagues paired strangers for conversations, then asked each person how much they liked their partner and how much they thought their partner liked them. Consistently, people underestimated how much they were liked. The researchers called this the liking gap.\n\nAfter a chat, we pick over our own awkward moments, while our partner was busy worrying about theirs. The gap appeared in lab studies, among university students getting to know their housemates and in a workshop setting. Many of us leave conversations more pessimistic than we should be, which can make us hesitate to follow up and build new friendships."],
    ["Turning down the spotlight", "Remembering that others notice and judge you less than you fear frees you to speak up and try new things.",
      "The spotlight effect can keep us quiet in meetings or stop us trying a new dance class or wearing something different. Knowing about it offers practical freedom. Before turning down an opportunity because you fear looking foolish, remember the Barry Manilow shirt: far fewer people will notice than you imagine, and fewer still will care tomorrow.\n\nWhen you replay a mistake, ask how much you remember of someone else's similar slip last week. Probably very little. After meeting someone new, assume they liked you more than your inner critic suggests, and consider sending that follow-up message. The people around you are not your audience. They are the stars of their own shows."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In the Barry Manilow T-shirt study, how did the wearers' guesses compare with reality?", ["Wearers guessed about half noticed; about a quarter could say who was on it","Wearers guessed a quarter noticed; about half could say who was on it","Wearers guessed nobody noticed; nearly everyone could say who was on it","Wearers guessed almost everyone noticed; and almost everyone had"], 0, "People overestimate how much others notice them, which is the spotlight effect."],
    [1, "Why do we tend to overestimate how closely others are watching us?", ["We rely on other people's reactions and adjust too much","We start from our own intense inner view and don't adjust enough","We assume strangers are paying close attention to everyone","We forget our own mistakes and notice theirs"], 1, "Our inner experience feels vivid to us, while others are mostly absorbed in themselves."],
    [2, "What happened when students were told about the illusion of transparency before giving a speech?", ["They felt more anxious but spoke for longer","They felt less anxious and their speeches were rated better","They felt calmer but their speeches were rated no better","They avoided speaking altogether"], 1, "Knowing your nerves are less visible than you fear helps break the cycle of worry."],
    [3, "What does the liking gap show?", ["People tend to underestimate how much a new partner liked them","People tend to overestimate how much a new partner liked them","People tend to like strangers less than their friends","People tend to like others less as a conversation goes on"], 0, "We dwell on our own awkward moments, so we leave conversations more pessimistic than we should be."],
    [4, "What is a sensible response to fearing you'll look foolish?", ["Avoid situations where you might make a mistake","Remember that others notice less and forget faster","Apologise at length to everyone who might have noticed","Assume the people around you are judging closely"], 1, "Few people notice your slips, and fewer remember them, so the fear is usually bigger than the risk."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"You think everyone saw your slip. A famous T-shirt study says they didn't."});

WB.L("deja-vu", "psychology", "intermediate", "Deja vu and other glitches of memory",
  "That eerie sense that you've lived this exact moment before. Is it a glimpse of the future, a past life, or something stranger and more ordinary?",
  [
    ["Familiar but false", "Deja vu is a strong sense of familiarity combined with the awareness that it is wrong.",
      "You walk into a cafe you have never visited, and everything feels oddly known: the light, the clatter of cups, the conversation next door. For a few seconds you feel certain you have lived this moment, while knowing you can't have. The French phrase means 'already seen'. Surveys suggest roughly two thirds of people have experienced it. It is most common in teenagers and young adults, becomes less frequent with age, and has been linked with tiredness and stress.\n\nWhat makes it special is the clash: part of your mind says 'I know this' while another says 'no, you don't'. Psychologist Chris Moulin argues this clash is the heart of the experience."],
    ["Forgotten layouts", "A new scene can feel familiar because its layout matches one you've forgotten, but the sense of knowing what comes next is an illusion.",
      "Psychologist Anne Cleary at Colorado State University proposed that deja vu can occur when a new scene resembles something in your past that you can't consciously recall. In virtual reality experiments, new scenes with a similar spatial layout to earlier ones were more likely to trigger deja vu when people could not recall the earlier scene. That unfamiliar cafe may share its arrangement of tables, windows and counter with a room you forgot years ago.\n\nMany people also feel they can predict what comes next. In a 2018 test, participants felt sure which way to turn, but their predictions were no better than chance. The premonition is a feeling, not foresight."],
    ["The brain checks itself", "Deja vu may be your brain's error-checking at work, a sign of a healthy memory system.",
      "Researchers can trigger something like deja vu in the lab. People hear related words such as bed, pillow, night and dream, but never the word sleep. Asked if they heard a word starting with 's', they know they did not, yet 'sleep' feels oddly familiar.\n\nIn a study presented in 2016, Akira O'Connor and colleagues scanned brains during these moments. Activity showed up mainly in frontal areas involved in decision-making and conflict, rather than in the memory hubs. One interpretation is that deja vu reflects the brain checking its memories for errors. If so, it may be a sign of a healthy memory system doing its job."],
    ["When it's a medical clue", "Occasional deja vu is harmless, but very frequent or prolonged episodes with other symptoms are worth a GP visit.",
      "For most people, deja vu is harmless and occasional. It does have a known link with temporal lobe epilepsy, where some people experience intense deja vu as an aura, a warning sensation at the start of a seizure. Surgeons stimulating parts of the temporal lobe during operations have also triggered deja vu-like feelings, which helped researchers pin down where the sensation arises.\n\nSigns that it might need checking include happening very often, lasting a long time, or coming with blank spells, strange smells or tastes, or confusion. This is general information rather than medical advice. If you are concerned, talk to your GP. healthdirect also has information."],
    ["Jamais vu and its cousins", "Deja vu has an opposite, jamais vu, and together with tip-of-the-tongue states it shows memory is an active, self-monitoring process.",
      "Deja vu has a stranger opposite called jamais vu, 'never seen': the sense that something you know well, such as a familiar word or face, suddenly seems foreign. You can trigger a version by writing a common word like 'door' over and over until it looks wrong. In 2023, Chris Moulin, Akira O'Connor and colleagues won an Ig Nobel Prize, the award for research that first makes people laugh and then think, for studying this.\n\nAnother familiar glitch is the tip-of-the-tongue state, when you are sure you know a name but can't retrieve it, often recalling its first letter or number of syllables. Together, these quirks reveal memory as a busy, self-monitoring process, not a simple recording."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What distinguishes deja vu from an ordinary false memory?", ["It only ever happens during vivid dreams","You feel familiarity while knowing it can't be right","It only happens in places you have really visited","It happens only once or twice in a lifetime"], 1, "Deja vu is the clash between a feeling of knowing and the awareness that the feeling is wrong."],
    [1, "According to Anne Cleary's research, what can trigger deja vu?", ["Strong sunlight in a place you have never been","Hearing your own name called out in a crowd","A new scene whose layout matches one you've forgotten","Eating a food you haven't tasted in many years"], 2, "A familiar spatial layout can create real familiarity even when you can't recall its source."],
    [2, "What might deja vu say about your memory?", ["A sign that your memory is starting to fail","A sign your brain is checking its memory for errors","A sign that you have lived the moment before","A sign that you are probably dreaming now"], 1, "Brain scans suggest deja vu involves decision and conflict areas, consistent with a memory system checking itself."],
    [3, "Which situation suggests deja vu might be worth discussing with a GP?", ["It happened once during a holiday last year","It tends to come with tiredness and stress","It is frequent or long-lasting, or brings confusion","It happens in a place like somewhere you've been"], 2, "Frequent or prolonged deja vu with other symptoms can be linked with temporal lobe epilepsy, so get it checked."],
    [4, "What is jamais vu?", ["Remembering a vivid scene from a past life","Seeing the very same scene twice in one day","A familiar word or face suddenly seeming foreign","Forgetting most of a dream on waking up"], 2, "Jamais vu is the opposite of deja vu and shows memory is an active, self-monitoring process."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Two thirds of us have felt it, yet it may be your brain doing its job."});

WB.L("boredom-science", "psychology", "beginner", "Boredom: the feeling we'll do almost anything to escape",
  "Given the choice between sitting quietly with their thoughts and giving themselves an electric shock, many people chose the shock. What is boredom trying to tell us?",
  [
    ["Shock or sit still?", "Many people find being alone with their thoughts so unpleasant that they would rather give themselves a mild electric shock.",
      "In studies published in 2014, psychologist Timothy Wilson and colleagues at the University of Virginia asked people to sit alone in a plain room for about six to fifteen minutes with nothing to do but think. No phone, no book, no pen. Many found it unpleasant.\n\nIn one version, participants had first been given a mild electric shock, and many said they would pay money to avoid feeling it again. Yet when left alone with a button that would deliver the same shock, about two thirds of men and a quarter of women pressed it at least once. One participant pressed it 190 times. The result raised a question: what exactly is boredom?"],
    ["A signal, not a flaw", "Boredom is the discomfort of wanting to engage but being unable to, and it works as a signal to change course.",
      "Boredom is not the same as having nothing to do. You can relax happily on a beach, and you can be bored stiff in a meeting while busy. In 2012, psychologist John Eastwood and colleagues defined it as 'the aversive experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity'. The mind is ready to be used, but the situation, perhaps too easy, too hard or meaningless, offers nothing to capture it.\n\nMany researchers now see boredom as a useful emotion, like hunger or pain. Andreas Elpidorou describes it as a regulatory signal that nudges us towards goals that matter. It is not laziness or a moral failing."],
    ["Boredom and creativity", "Some mind-wandering time may help creativity, but boredom is not a guaranteed booster.",
      "A popular idea says boredom makes us more creative. In a 2014 study, Sandi Mann and Rebekah Cadman had some participants copy numbers from a phone book. Afterwards, they thought of more uses for a pair of plastic cups than people who had not been bored. The theory is that when the world is dull, the mind wanders and makes unexpected connections.\n\nIt is an appealing finding, but the studies have been small and results across experiments have been mixed. Some mind-wandering time may help creativity, but treating boredom as a guaranteed creativity booster goes beyond the evidence."],
    ["Proneness and phones", "How we respond to boredom matters, and reaching for a screen may silence the signal without satisfying it.",
      "People differ in how often they get bored. Since Richard Farmer and Norman Sundberg developed a Boredom Proneness Scale in 1986, researchers have found that easily bored people report more attention problems, impulsive behaviour and low mood. Boredom proneness has also been linked with gambling, overeating and substance use, perhaps because these offer quick stimulation. These are associations, not proof that boredom causes the problems.\n\nSmartphones offer an instant escape from even a few seconds of boredom. If boredom signals us to seek something meaningful, endless scrolling may silence the signal without satisfying it. Whether phones make us more bored overall is not settled, but a mindless scroll rarely leaves people refreshed."],
    ["Making boredom work", "When bored, ask what is missing (challenge, meaning, novelty or control) and respond, or sometimes just let yourself be bored.",
      "If boredom is a signal, listen to it. Ask what is missing: challenge, meaning, novelty or control. A task that is too easy can become a game, such as beating your previous time. A task that is too hard may need breaking into smaller steps. If something feels pointless, connect it to a goal you care about.\n\nIt is also worth occasionally letting yourself be bored without escaping, such as walking without headphones or sitting with a cup of tea and no screen. Many people find that ideas, plans and solutions surface in those quiet gaps. Boredom, used well, can be the start of something interesting."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the 2014 study find about being alone with one's thoughts?", ["Many found it unpleasant, and some chose to shock themselves","Most found it relaxing and wanted more time alone","Most preferred reading to sitting, but none chose a shock","Few found it unpleasant, and nobody chose a shock"], 0, "Many people dislike unoccupied time enough to pick a mild shock over quiet thinking."],
    [1, "How do researchers now often view boredom?", ["As a sign of laziness or weak willpower","As a signal that your activity isn't engaging you","As a feeling that only happens with nothing to do","As a harmless feeling that serves no purpose"], 1, "Like hunger, boredom nudges us to change course towards something more meaningful."],
    [2, "How strong is the evidence that boredom boosts creativity?", ["Conclusive, backed by many large and careful studies","Mixed, from small studies, so 'guaranteed' overstates it","Disproved by every single experiment run so far","Strong, but only for physical rather than mental tasks"], 1, "Mind-wandering may help, but results are mixed, so boredom is no guaranteed creativity booster."],
    [3, "Why might constant phone scrolling leave us restless?", ["It satisfies the need for meaningful activity too well","It makes us feel bored for the rest of the day","It mutes the signal without giving us anything real","It stops our minds from wandering completely"], 2, "A mindless scroll escapes the feeling without answering what the signal was asking for."],
    [4, "What is a useful response to boredom?", ["Distract yourself immediately every time","Treat boredom as a personal failing","Do whatever seems easiest in that moment","Ask what is missing and adjust the task"], 3, "Identifying whether challenge, meaning, novelty or control is missing lets you fix the mismatch."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Many people chose an electric shock over sitting quietly. What does that say?"});

WB.L("choice-overload", "psychology", "intermediate", "The paradox of choice: can more options make us less happy?",
  "Twenty-four jams, hundreds of streaming shows, dozens of super funds. More choice sounds like freedom, so why does it sometimes leave us stuck?",
  [
    ["The jam study", "A famous study found shoppers stopped for a big jam display but bought far more from a small one.",
      "In a California supermarket, psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper set up a tasting booth for gourmet jams. Some days it offered 24 flavours, others just six. The big display drew more shoppers to stop. But when it came to buying, the result flipped: about three per cent of those at the large display bought a jar, against about thirty per cent at the small one. Published in 2000, it was cited in business books, TED talks and marketing meetings.\n\nIn 2004, Barry Schwartz built on it in The Paradox of Choice, arguing that beyond a point, more options leave us less satisfied: more regret, higher expectations and more self-blame."],
    ["Then came replications", "Later research found choice overload is not a general law: it depends on how hard the options are to navigate.",
      "As researchers tried to repeat choice overload experiments, the picture grew messier. In 2010, Benjamin Scheibehenne, Rainer Greifeneder and Peter Todd pooled about fifty experiments. On average, the effect of the number of options was close to zero. Some studies found overload, some found more options led to more buying, and many found no difference.\n\nA 2015 analysis by Alexander Chernev, Ulf Bockenholt and Joseph Goodman found overload was more likely when options were complex and hard to compare, decisions were difficult or time-pressured, or people had no clear preferences. If you already love apricot jam, a huge range is no problem. One striking study is a starting point for science, not the final word."],
    ["Maximisers and satisficers", "Satisficers, who choose the first option that is good enough, tend to be happier than maximisers who must find the best.",
      "Economist Herbert Simon coined 'satisficing': choosing the first option that meets your standards rather than searching for the absolute best. Schwartz and colleagues contrasted satisficers with maximisers, who feel they must examine every option. In a 2002 study, high maximisers reported more regret and less happiness and life satisfaction.\n\nA later study of university graduates found maximisers tended to land jobs with higher starting salaries, but felt less satisfied with them. Being a satisficer does not mean having low standards. It means knowing what is good enough for you, then stopping, rather than chasing a perfect option that may not exist."],
    ["Big decisions and defaults", "Overload bites hardest on big, complex decisions like super, where people often delay or accept the default.",
      "Choice overload matters most for complex decisions such as insurance, energy plans and retirement savings. Research by Iyengar and colleagues on American retirement plans found that as the number of investment funds offered rose, participation tended to dip slightly. When overwhelmed, people often delay or accept the default.\n\nIn Australia, many workers never actively choose a super fund, and their contributions go into a default fund, which is part of why rules about default products matter. This is general information, not financial advice. To compare super funds, energy plans or insurance, government tools such as Moneysmart and the ATO's YourSuper comparison tool can narrow the field."],
    ["Choosing well", "Decide what matters first, limit your search, satisfice on small decisions and stop shopping once you've chosen.",
      "You can protect yourself from the downsides of abundant choice. First, decide what matters before you look: write down two or three must-haves and ignore options that fail them. Second, limit your search, perhaps to three options or a time limit. Third, for small decisions such as toothpaste or tonight's show, deliberately satisfice: pick something good enough and move on.\n\nFourth, once you have chosen, stop shopping around, because constantly checking alternatives keeps regret alive. Finally, use trusted recommendations and comparison tools to do some of the sorting. You don't have to experience every option to enjoy choice."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the jam study find?", ["More shoppers bought from the 24-flavour display than the six-flavour one","Shoppers stopped at the big display but far fewer bought there","Shoppers ignored both displays equally","Shoppers preferred tasting to buying at both displays"], 1, "The large display attracted attention but about three per cent bought, against about thirty per cent at the small display."],
    [1, "What did pooling about fifty choice overload experiments show?", ["More choice always lowers how much people buy","The average effect of options on buying was close to zero","More choice always raises how much people buy","Choice overload disappears when people are experts"], 1, "The jam finding is real for some situations, but 'more choice is bad' is not a general law."],
    [2, "What does being a satisficer mean?", ["Having low standards for what you will accept","Choosing at random and never looking back","Examining every option to find the very best","Picking the first option that meets your standards"], 3, "Satisficers know what is good enough for them, which tends to bring more satisfaction and less regret."],
    [3, "What do people often do when overwhelmed by a complex decision like choosing a super fund?", ["Compare every available fund in detail","Choose the most expensive option","Ask a friend to decide","Delay or accept the default"], 3, "Overwhelmed people often postpone or accept defaults, which is why comparison tools and default rules matter."],
    [4, "Which habit best protects against choice overload?", ["Keep browsing alternatives after you've chosen","Decide your must-haves first and limit your search","Always look at every option","Choose the most popular item without checking"], 1, "Clear criteria and a limited search keep the options manageable."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"24 jams or six? Why a famous supermarket result is more complicated than it looks."});

WB.L("self-control-marshmallow", "psychology", "advanced", "Willpower: what the marshmallow test really tells us",
  "A child, a marshmallow and a promise of two if they can wait. It became psychology's most famous test of self control, and the real story is more interesting than the legend.",
  [
    ["One treat now, or two", "The marshmallow test began as a study of strategies for delaying gratification, and was later spun into a story about predicting success.",
      "In the late 1960s and early 1970s, psychologist Walter Mischel and colleagues ran experiments at a Stanford University nursery school. A child sat at a table with a treat such as a marshmallow, pretzel or biscuit. If they waited until the researcher returned, they got two treats. If not, they could ring a bell and eat the single treat straight away. Some children gobbled it immediately; others squirmed, sang or covered their eyes.\n\nMischel's original aim was to learn which strategies helped children delay. A 1990 follow-up of teenagers found longer waiters scored higher on the SAT. The legend grew, though the samples were small and drawn from a privileged university community."],
    ["The 2018 replication", "In a larger, more diverse sample, the link between waiting and later success was half as strong and mostly disappeared after accounting for family background.",
      "In 2018, Tyler Watts, Greg Duncan and Haonan Quan revisited the idea with a much larger, more diverse sample of several hundred children. Waiting longer at age four was linked with better academic achievement at fifteen, but only about half as strongly as in the original.\n\nMore importantly, once they accounted for family background, home environment and early cognitive skills, the link shrank substantially and much of it disappeared. A lot of what the test seemed to measure might reflect a child's circumstances rather than a stable inner trait of willpower. The test still tells us something, but far less than the legend implied."],
    ["Is waiting always smart?", "Whether waiting makes sense depends on trust: children who had been let down by an adult waited far less.",
      "A 2013 study by Celeste Kidd and colleagues at the University of Rochester added a twist. Before the marshmallow test, children were promised better art supplies. For one group, the researcher returned with them. For the other, the researcher came back empty-handed with an apology. Children who had met a reliable adult waited, on average, around four times longer.\n\nIf you grow up where promises are often broken and resources can disappear, grabbing what you can now may be a sensible strategy, not a lack of self-control. Decisions about waiting depend on trust and circumstance, not only on character."],
    ["The willpower battery", "The idea that willpower is a limited resource that drains like a battery has largely failed to replicate.",
      "Ego depletion was another hugely influential idea. In a 1998 study, Roy Baumeister and colleagues asked hungry participants to resist freshly baked cookies and eat radishes instead. The radish eaters then gave up sooner on an unsolvable puzzle than the cookie eaters. The conclusion was that willpower is a limited resource, like a muscle that tires.\n\nHundreds of studies followed. Then in 2016, a coordinated replication across more than twenty labs and over two thousand participants found an effect close to zero. A later large project led by Kathleen Vohs, one of the original proponents, also found at most a very small effect. The strong willpower-battery version is now widely doubted."],
    ["Strategies, not strength", "Self-control works best through strategies like removing temptations, if-then plans and habits, and kindness after lapses.",
      "Mischel's original research was about strategies. Children who distracted themselves, looked away or imagined the marshmallow as a fluffy cloud waited longer than those who stared at it. A 2012 study by Wilhelm Hofmann and colleagues found people with high self-control reported fewer temptations overall. They arranged their lives so they rarely had to resist. Angela Duckworth stresses situational strategies: remove temptations and make good choices easier.\n\nPeter Gollwitzer's if-then plans help too: 'If I want a snack after dinner, I will have fruit.' So do habits and kindness after lapses, since harsh self-criticism can make people quit. Self-control depends on strategies, environments and trust, all of which can change."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the original aim of Mischel's marshmallow experiments?", ["To predict which children would succeed in life","To find which strategies help children delay","To test if children prefer sweet or savoury treats","To measure each child's intelligence"], 1, "The prediction story came later; the original work was about the strategies that help children wait."],
    [1, "What did the 2018 replication suggest about waiting time and later achievement?", ["The link was stronger than in the original","The link halved, and mostly vanished after adjustment","There was no link at all, even before adjusting","The link held only for wealthy families"], 1, "Much of what the test measured seems to reflect circumstances rather than a fixed trait of willpower."],
    [2, "Why did children who had been let down by the researcher wait less?", ["They disliked the treats","They didn't understand the instructions","They had less reason to trust the payoff","They were naturally less patient"], 2, "Where promises are broken, taking the sure thing now can be a sensible choice."],
    [3, "What did large replications find about ego depletion, the idea that willpower runs down like a battery?", ["A strong effect confirmed by every lab","An effect close to zero in large replications","An effect found only in young children","An effect that reversed in older adults"], 1, "Multi-lab replications failed to find the strong willpower-battery effect."],
    [4, "What do people with high self-control seem to do?", ["Resist temptations through constant effort","Avoid all treats and enjoyment","Arrange their lives to face fewer temptations","Rarely feel any desires at all"], 2, "Self-control is often about smart design of your environment rather than raw strength."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The famous marshmallow test says less than the legend claims. Here's what it shows."});

